<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Uniform continuity: reading after the epsilon-delta section. Lean version:          -->
<!--   1. The x^2 scratch work from 0.6, displayed; its delta depends on a; the explorer.  -->
<!--   2. Continuity on A with "for all a in A" boxed; uniform continuity moves the box     -->
<!--      past "there exists delta".                                                       -->
<!--   3. x^2 on [2,5]: the same scratch work with 2|a|+1 <= 11, so delta = min{1,eps/11}. -->
<!--   4. x^2 on R: the negation (box becomes "there exist a, x"), eps = 1, a = 1/delta.   -->
<!-- The explorer is assets/interactives/epsilon_delta_explorer.html. The part of the    -->
<!-- iframe address after # sets it up (function, A, a, eps, which boxes are checked);   -->
<!-- "embed=1" gives the compact layout. The print version shows the image in           -->
<!-- assets/images/uc_explorer_preview.png and a QR code.                                -->
<section xml:id="sec-uniform_continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Uniform Continuity</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-uc-x-squared">
    <title>How <m>\delta</m> depends on <m>a</m></title>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="sec-epsilon_delta"/>, we proved that <m>f(x)=x^2</m> is continuous at every <m>a\in\R</m>. Here is the scratch work again. When <m>|x-a|\lt 1</m>,
      <md>
        <mrow>|x^2-a^2| \amp= |x-a|\cdot|x+a|</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp\le |x-a|\cdot\bigl(|x-a|+2|a|\bigr) \amp\amp \text{(triangle inequality)}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp\le (2|a|+1)\,|x-a| \amp\amp \text{(since } |x-a|\lt 1\text{)}.</mrow>
      </md>
      This is less than <m>\epsilon</m> when also <m>|x-a|\lt\epsilon/(2|a|+1)</m>, so we chose
      <md>\delta=\min\left\{1,\frac{\epsilon}{2|a|+1}\right\}.</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      This <m>\delta</m> depends on <m>a</m>. For <m>\epsilon=1</m>, it is <m>1/5</m> at <m>a=2</m>, <m>1/11</m> at <m>a=5</m>, and <m>1/201</m> at <m>a=100</m>. The farther out <m>a</m> is, the steeper the graph, and the closer <m>x</m> must stay to <m>a</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The explorer shows the same thing. For <m>\epsilon=1</m>, it finds the largest <m>\delta</m> that works at each <m>a</m> in <m>A=[2,5]</m> and graphs it at the bottom. Press the play button to sweep <m>a</m> across <m>A</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-uc-explorer">
      <caption>The <m>\epsilon</m>–<m>\delta</m> explorer for <m>f(x)=x^2</m> with <m>\epsilon=1</m>.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="uc_explorer" iframe="interactives/epsilon_delta_explorer.html#embed=1&amp;f=x%5E2&amp;S=2,5&amp;a=3.5&amp;eps=1&amp;auto=1&amp;graph=1&amp;onlyS=1&amp;view=1.5,5.5,-1,28" width="100%" aspect="600:975" preview="images/uc_explorer_preview.png">
        <shortdescription>The epsilon-delta explorer for f of x equals x squared on A equals the interval from 2 to 5: a graph with the epsilon band and the largest delta window at a point a, and below it a graph of the largest delta at every point of A.</shortdescription>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-explore">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the explorer with <m>\epsilon=1</m>.
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Where on <m>A=[2,5]</m> is the largest <m>\delta</m> smallest? About how big is it there?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Change <m>A</m> to <m>[2,20]</m>, then to <m>[2,100]</m>. What happens to the smallest value? What does this suggest for <m>A=\R</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          (a) At <m>a=5</m>, where it is about <m>0.101</m>. So one <m>\delta</m>, such as <m>0.1</m>, works at every point of <m>[2,5]</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          (b) It shrinks: about <m>0.025</m> on <m>[2,20]</m> and <m>0.005</m> on <m>[2,100]</m>. This suggests that on <m>\R</m>, no single <m>\delta\gt 0</m> works.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-uc-definition">
    <title>One <m>\delta</m> for every point</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>A\subseteq\operatorname{dom}(f)</m>. Written with every quantifier, <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>A</m> if
      <md>
        <mrow>\boxed{\forall\, a\in A}\ \forall\,\epsilon\gt 0\ \exists\,\delta\gt 0 \text{ s.t.}</mrow>
        <mrow>\forall\, x\in A \text{ with } |x-a|\lt\delta,\ |f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon.</mrow>
      </md>
      Since <m>a</m> comes before <m>\delta</m>, <m>\delta</m> may depend on <m>a</m>, as it does for <m>x^2</m>. Uniform continuity moves the box past <m>\exists\,\delta</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-uniformly-continuous">
      <title>Uniform continuity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A\subseteq\operatorname{dom}(f)</m>. The function <m>f</m> is <term>uniformly continuous on <m>A</m></term>
          <idx><h>uniformly continuous</h></idx>
          if
          <md>
            <mrow>\forall\,\epsilon\gt 0\ \exists\,\delta\gt 0 \text{ s.t. } \boxed{\forall\, a\in A}</mrow>
            <mrow>\forall\, x\in A \text{ with } |x-a|\lt\delta,\ |f(x)-f(a)|\lt\epsilon.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Now <m>\delta</m> is chosen before <m>a</m>, so one <m>\delta</m> must work at every point of <m>A</m>. For example, <m>f(x)=3x+5</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>\R</m>, because the proof of <xref ref="prop-linear-continuous"/> chose <m>\delta=\epsilon/3</m>, which does not depend on <m>a</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-uc-on-interval">
    <title><m>x^2</m> on <m>[2,5]</m></title>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-x2-uc-interval">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)=x^2</m>. Then <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[2,5]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-uc-interval-scratch">
      <title>Scratch work</title>
      <p>
        Every <m>a\in[2,5]</m> has <m>|a|\le 5</m>, so the scratch work above gives, when <m>|x-a|\lt 1</m>,
        <md>|x^2-a^2|\le(2|a|+1)\,|x-a|\le 11\,|x-a|.</md>
        This is less than <m>\epsilon</m> when also <m>|x-a|\lt\epsilon/11</m>. So <m>\delta=\min\{1,\epsilon/11\}</m>, the <m>\delta</m> from the formula at <m>a=5</m>, works at every <m>a\in[2,5]</m>. It does not depend on <m>a</m>, so the proof can choose it before <m>a</m> appears.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <proof ref="prop-x2-uc-interval">
      <p>
        We must prove that for all <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, there exists <m>\delta\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>a,x\in[2,5]</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>, <m>|x^2-a^2|\lt\epsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>. Choose <m>\delta=\min\{1,\epsilon/11\}</m>, which is positive. Let <m>a,x\in[2,5]</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>. We must show that <m>|x^2-a^2|\lt\epsilon</m>. Since <m>|x-a|\lt\delta\le 1</m> and <m>|a|\le 5</m>, we have <m>|x+a|\le|x-a|+2|a|\lt 11</m>. Since also <m>|x-a|\lt\delta\le\epsilon/11</m>,
        <md>|x^2-a^2|=|x-a|\cdot|x+a|\le 11\,|x-a|\lt\epsilon,</md>
        as desired.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-uc-not-on-R">
    <title><m>x^2</m> on <m>\R</m></title>

    <p>
      On <m>\R</m>, the <m>\delta</m>'s from the formula shrink to <m>0</m> as <m>a</m> grows. To prove that no single <m>\delta</m> works, prove the negation of uniform continuity:
      <md>\exists\,\epsilon\gt 0 \text{ s.t. } \forall\,\delta\gt 0\ \boxed{\exists\, a,x\in A} \text{ with } |x-a|\lt\delta \text{ s.t. } |f(x)-f(a)|\ge\epsilon.</md>
      The points <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> come after <m>\delta</m>, so they may depend on <m>\delta</m>.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-x2-not-uc-R">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)=x^2</m>. Then <m>f</m> is not uniformly continuous on <m>\R</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-uc-R-scratch">
      <title>Scratch work</title>
      <p>
        Take <m>\epsilon=1</m>. Given <m>\delta\gt 0</m>, put <m>x=a+\delta/2</m>, so <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m>. For <m>a\gt 0</m>,
        <md>x^2-a^2=(x-a)(x+a)=\frac{\delta}{2}\left(2a+\frac{\delta}{2}\right)\gt a\delta.</md>
        This is at least <m>1</m> once <m>a\ge 1/\delta</m>, so choose <m>a=1/\delta</m>, far enough out that the graph is steep.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <proof ref="prop-x2-not-uc-R">
      <p>
        We prove the negation: there exists <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m> such that for all <m>\delta\gt 0</m>, there exist <m>a,x\in\R</m> with <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m> such that <m>|x^2-a^2|\ge\epsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose <m>\epsilon=1</m>. Let <m>\delta\gt 0</m>. Choose <m>a=1/\delta</m> and <m>x=1/\delta+\delta/2</m>. We must show that <m>|x-a|\lt\delta</m> and <m>|x^2-a^2|\ge 1</m>. First, <m>|x-a|=\delta/2\lt\delta</m>. Second, <m>x-a</m> and <m>x+a</m> are positive, so
        <md>|x^2-a^2|=(x-a)(x+a)=\frac{\delta}{2}\left(\frac{2}{\delta}+\frac{\delta}{2}\right)=1+\frac{\delta^2}{4}\ge 1,</md>
        as desired.
      </p>
    </proof>

    <p>
      So <m>x^2</m> is continuous at every point of <m>\R</m> and uniformly continuous on <m>[2,5]</m>, but not uniformly continuous on <m>\R</m>. Ross proves that a function that is continuous on a closed, bounded interval is uniformly continuous there (Theorem 19.2).
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
